# Cursor Rules for Element Delete Chrome Extension

## Project Overview

This is a Chrome Extension project that allows users to visually delete elements from webpages. The extension uses Manifest V3 and provides a clean, intuitive interface for element selection and deletion.

## Code Style & Standards

### JavaScript

- Use ES6+ features (const/let, arrow functions, template literals)
- Follow camelCase naming convention for variables and functions
- Use descriptive names that clearly indicate purpose
- Add JSDoc comments for all public functions and classes
- Implement proper error handling with try-catch blocks
- Use async/await for asynchronous operations

### CSS

- Use CSS custom properties (variables) for consistent theming
- Implement BEM methodology for class naming
- Use flexbox and grid for layouts
- Ensure all interactive elements have hover/focus states
- Implement smooth transitions for better UX
- Use CSS-in-JS or scoped styles to prevent conflicts

### HTML

- Use semantic HTML elements
- Include proper ARIA labels for accessibility
- Keep markup clean and minimal
- Use data attributes for JavaScript hooks

## Architecture Principles

### Separation of Concerns

- **ElementSelector**: Handle element detection, highlighting, and selection
- **SidePanel**: Manage the control panel UI and user interactions
- **ElementManager**: Handle DOM manipulation, deletion, and undo operations
- **EventManager**: Coordinate messaging between components
- **StateManager**: Track extension state and user selections

### Modularity

- Each component should be self-contained with clear interfaces
- Use event-driven architecture for component communication
- Implement dependency injection where appropriate
- Keep functions small and focused on single responsibilities

### Performance

- Use event delegation for dynamic elements
- Implement debouncing for mouse move events
- Cache DOM queries where possible
- Clean up event listeners and timers on deactivation
- Use requestAnimationFrame for smooth animations

## Chrome Extension Specific Rules

### Manifest V3 Compliance

- Use service workers instead of background pages
- Implement proper permission handling
- Use content scripts for webpage injection
- Follow security best practices

### Content Script Isolation

- Use shadow DOM for UI components to prevent style conflicts
- Implement proper message passing between content script and background
- Handle webpage context changes gracefully
- Avoid global variable pollution

### User Experience

- Provide immediate visual feedback for all actions
- Implement smooth transitions and animations
- Ensure the extension doesn't interfere with normal webpage functionality
- Provide clear error messages and recovery options

## File Organization

### Core Files

- `manifest.json`: Extension configuration
- `background.js`: Service worker for background tasks
- `content.js`: Main content script with all functionality
- `panel.css`: Styles for the side panel and visual indicators

### Supporting Files

- `popup.html/js`: Extension popup interface
- `icons/`: Extension icons in multiple sizes
- `README.md`: Project documentation and setup instructions

## Testing & Quality

### Code Quality

- Write self-documenting code with clear variable names
- Implement proper error boundaries and fallbacks
- Add console logging for debugging (remove in production)
- Use TypeScript-like JSDoc for type safety

### Browser Compatibility

- Test on multiple Chrome versions
- Ensure functionality works on various website types
- Handle edge cases gracefully (empty pages, iframes, etc.)
- Implement progressive enhancement

## Security Considerations

### Content Security

- Validate all user inputs and DOM manipulations
- Use safe DOM methods to prevent XSS
- Implement proper sanitization for any user-generated content
- Follow Chrome extension security best practices

### Data Handling

- Minimize data collection and storage
- Implement proper cleanup on extension deactivation
- Use secure storage methods for any persistent data
- Handle sensitive webpage content appropriately

## Documentation Requirements

### Code Comments

- Document all public APIs and interfaces
- Explain complex algorithms and business logic
- Include usage examples for reusable components
- Document any browser-specific workarounds

### User Documentation

- Provide clear setup and installation instructions
- Include troubleshooting guides for common issues
- Document keyboard shortcuts and accessibility features
- Explain any limitations or known issues
